    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d356">

                <pb facs="0452" n="354" sameAs="frus:frus1932v04/pg354"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">793.94 Commission/542: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva (Gilbert) to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1932-11-21T15:00:00+01:00">November 21, 1932—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 21—1:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>328. Lytton report considered in Council meeting this morning. With the exception
                    of the President who gave a brief résumé of status of the question the only
                    speaker was the Japanese representative who reiterated substantially the
                    arguments contained in the printed observations of Japanese Government, a
                    document comprising 39 foolscap pages circulated by the Secretariat yesterday.
                    Copies are being forwarded by mail.<anchor n="25" xml:id="d356-f1"/> Consider it
                    probable that the Department has already received the text of the Japanese
                    observations from a Japanese source. If not, and summary is desired, please
                    instruct.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gilbert</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
